All 11 tests pass, including the previously failing month-end, leap-day, and year-end rollovers and `periodEnd('2026-01-31') === '2026-02-14'`.

## What was wrong

`src/dates.js` had two defects in its hand-rolled date arithmetic:

1. **`addDays` never carried into the next month/year.** It computed `day = day + days` and then clamped with `Math.min(day, daysInMonth(year, month))` — so adding 14 days to `2026-01-31` produced `2026-02-14`'s day number written in January (`2026-01-14`), and any crossing of a month or year boundary was wrong.
2. **`daysInMonth`'s leap rule was incomplete**: `(year % 4 === 0 && year % 100 !== 0)` misses century years divisible by 400 (e.g. 2000 would have been treated as a 28-day February).

## The fix

Since `parseIsoDate` already yields a real UTC `Date`, `addDays` now does the addition on the UTC `Date` itself and renders it back with `toIso`, so all calendar rollovers (month ends, Feb 29, Dec 31 → Jan 1, and negative steps) come from the calendar for free:

```js
export function addDays(iso, days) {
  const date = parseIsoDate(iso)
  if (!date) throw new Error('not an ISO date: ' + iso)
  date.setUTCDate(date.getUTCDate() + days)
  return toIso(date)
